ook at the history  of any of these software domains you  start to get a sense of it  so the initial challenge is about speed  to build stuff  it takes developers a long time to get  basic things done like provision an ec2  server or change what shows up in a text  box  tools come along that speed this up  dramatically so for example jquery makes  it stupidly easy to change what shows up  in the text box  by making it so easy to build stuff  these tools solve the initial problem  but they create another one which is  that now people build a lot of stuff  it becomes difficult to reason about all  this stuff what text boxes are expected  to appear on the page and what text are  those boxes expected to contain  so finally new tools arrive to tackle  this problem and these new tools are  built declaratively instead of  imperatively  that's because declarative tools are  highly effective at taming complexity  they do this in a couple main ways  so first they ask users to explicitly  state their intentions and expectations  which means you get an unambiguous plan  that's inspectable by anyone  second they provide a principled way of  managing change  instead of an unstructured logs of  things that you did you can ask the  system why did you make this change why  would you put this value inside this  text box and the system can give you a  clear answer  so now let's talk about a domain with  complexity and change of the wazoo  and that domain is data  in the world of data where are we on  this tooling journey  well it used to be very hard to build  stuff  10 years ago if you wanted to calculate  some statistic over every event on your  website you need to set up a compute  cluster with 20 different services and  even the simplest operations could take  tens of minutes to complete  since then we've built powerful  databases and data processing engines  that make this all effortless so now  with snowflake or spark you can write  three lines of code and get a result in  a cou