urce orchestration platform  we recently introduced software-defined  assets as a core element of dagster's  data model  with this new abstraction dexter becomes  a critical tool not just for running  workflows but also for organizing  managing and evolving data  a software-defined asset specifies an  asset that you intend to exist  as well as how to compute that asset's  contents  assets don't come out of nowhere they're  produced by computations which in  dagstar we call ups  to get a table in your data warehouse  you normally have to run some code that  puts it there  and those computations normally operate  on other assets  so a software defined asset is basically  the combination of these three things it  has an asset key which identifies the  asset that it corresponds to for example  the name of a table in a database or the  path to a file and a file system  it has an op which is the function that  can be run to produce the asset  it has upstream asset keys which  reference assets that we supply as  inputs to the op that we use to compute  our asset  and these upstream asset keys usually  correspond to asset keys of other  software defined assets which means that  a group of software-defined assets  naturally forms a graph  here's a software-defined asset that's  built using dagsor's python api  the name of the decorated function  defines the asset key so in this case  the asset represents a forecast of  activity on some website  the implementation of the decorated  function is the op  so in this case it uses pandas but ops  can use any compute framework and then  asset doesn't even need to be tabular  data for example it could be a machine  learning model or a set of images  the arguments to this function designate  the upstream assets that this asset is  based on in this case it's an asset  called activity daily stats which holds  historical data on our website's  activity  something you might notice here is that  i'm not showing any code that actually  reads or writes to or from physical  storage  the op is a pure function  to support this functional style of  programming dagstar allows the code that  writes an asset to storage to be  provided separately and shared by  multiple assets  it's not a requirement to do it that way  but it's preferred because it makes  assets more testable  but decorating a python function isn't  the only way to build a software-defined  asset so if you wanted to find an asset  using sql a dbt model actually fits all  the criteria that we talked about for a  software-defined asset  let's walk through one  this activity daily stats asset has the  same core components as the python asset  the name of the file which determines  the name of the dbt model is the asset  key  the select statement is the op it's what  we run to compute the contents of the  asset  and the refs are upstream asset keys  they point to assets that this  computation depends on  dexter can actually model dependencies  between assets defined in python and  assets to find in dbt  it does this by offering a python  utility that makes it easy to load all  the models in a dbt project as  software-defined assets  once you define a set of assets in code  either directly in python or by loading  them from an external tool you can start  working with them in dagster's web ui so  here's how you tell dexter to scan a  python package and make all the assets  inside of it  loadable from the web ui  and here's that web ui we have a visual  representation of all the assets that we  were just looking at in code  this is all open source by the way you  can get to this view by pip installing a  package and running a single command  so at the bottom we've got our activity  forecast asset which is the one that we  defined in python  right above that we've got the activity  daily stats asset which is the one that  we defined using dbt  and above that we've got a couple other  assets which came from models in our dbt  project  if you've worked with tools that show  dags this might look a little familiar  but there are a couple of key difference  off the bat that i'd like to point out  so the first is that each node  represents an asset not a task or up  in fact multiple assets can be generated  by the same op  these three dbt assets are generated by  the same run dbt op which invokes the  dbt command line tool  the second is that there's no central  dag artifact that holds the dependency  structure  each asset on its own knows about the  upstream assets that it depends on  this makes it scalable to large numbers  of assets you don't need a central team  to maintain a code artifact that  captures all of them  so here we're looking at just the assets  that i showed you the definitions of  we've also got links to assets that  these assets depend on but that are  loaded by dexter separately  so at this point all we're looking at  here is a set of asset definitions  they correspond to code in our git repo  not artifacts in physical storage that  is they have no associated  materializations  but dexter doesn't just track asset  definitions it also allows you to  materialize assets and tracks those  materializations  so we can click this button on the upper  right to materialize all the assets that  are shown here  that launches a run which contains a log  of everything that happens as we execute  the computations that materialize these  assets  when the run is complete each asset now  has a materialization  that is it now exists in persistent  storage and dexter knows about that and  when it arrived there  dexter knows some other useful stuff as  well and if we dive into one of these  assets we can see it  here's the page for an individual asset  our activity forecast asset that we  defined in python it contains details  about the asset and its history of  materializations  a materialization isn't just a record  saying that a computation ran it's a  description of the object that was  produced by the computation  dexter lets users plug in code that  records runtime metadata about the asset  that's being materialized  so for example we've recorded a few  things about this materialization we've  recorded the number of rows the file  system pathway it's stored a sample of  the data and the set of columns and  their data types  this asset focus view is especially  valuable when you shift to the  perspective of someone who depends on an  asset  so let's imagine that we're in our bi  tool we're using this asset to try to  understand what the future holds for our  website  we notice something fishy  maybe some of the data that we expect to  be there is missing  so we can pull up this search box in  dagstr  type in the asset that we care about and  get to the asset page that we were  looking at before  from that page we have all this  information about the asset its  materializations its dependencies its  metadata at our fingertips and if we  have permissions we can directly launch  a run to re-materialize the asset and  patch up the issue  so to summarize what we just saw there  software-defined assets provide a layer  above our physical storage that allows  us to express what assets we expect to  exist and how those assets are produced  the software-defined asset graph is  based on assets defined in code which  means that practices for managing and  evolving code can be harnessed for  managing data  we can launch runs to materialize our  assets which means computing their  contents and writing those contents to  physical storage  by focusing our tooling on assets  instead of tasks it becomes easy to get  visibility into what has happened with  the assets that we care about  so we define some assets and materialize  them which is cool but it's not enough  and it's not enough because assets are  constantly changing  new upstream data is constantly arriving  and we're constantly changing the code  that derives our assets from that  upstream data  the result is that our asset  materializations naturally drift from  their definitions over time  when that drift occurs we need to launch  computations to bring those  materializations in line with our  definitions  and launching computations at the right  job at the right time is typically the  job of an orchestrator  traditionally orchestrators are systems  that are responsible for a few things  they invoke computations at the right  time  they model the dependencies between  computations  and they track what computations ran  this makes orchestrators the natural  experts on a few topics  they know when stuff happens they know  when stuff is going to happen and they  know what it takes to make something  happen  and because they're experts on these  things they hold the key to answering  some of the most important questions  that we end up having about our assets  so for example is this asset up to date  what do i need to run to refresh this  asset  what code and data were used to generate  this asset  after pushing a change  what assets need to be updated  when will this asset be updated next  but if you try to pose these questions  to a traditional orchestrator you'll  immediately run into the issue they have  no idea what an asset is  they can tell you what tasks have  completed  what tasks are meant to run after each  other but not about how any of that has  to do with the assets that these tasks  are reading and writing  they're ill-equipped to answer these  questions because asset is not central  to their data model  if we could conceive of orchestration in  a way that's centered on assets we'd  have an extremely powerful set of tools  for managing and understanding them  so we talked about the job of a  traditional orchestrator the job of an  asset orchestrator is a little different  it's to manage change in data assets  dexter supports two approaches to  orchestration for assets  the first one looks very similar to how  scheduling works in traditional  orchestrators except that it focuses on  assets instead of tasks you can select a  set of assets re-materialized at some  regular cadence or via a sensor based on  arbitrary logic  the second one fully leans into the  declarative approach you tell your  orchestrator to launch computations that  resolve discrepancies between your asset  definitions and their materializations  in storage  so let's start with a  one on the left as a way to get our feet  wet  this code tells dagster to run a job  every day at midnight and that job  materializes a subset of the assets in  our asset graph so it targets assets not  tasks  here's what this scheduled job looks  like in dagstr's ui so up on the top  here we can see when it's going to run  and the job includes just the assets  that we've selected we can still see the  data lineage between the assets inside  the job and the assets outside the job  putting the schedule on the asset  instead of on a task makes it easy for  stakeholders to answer questions about  when assets will be updated next  when they navigate to the page for an  asset they can see the frequency that  it's going to be updated at  so just by framing scheduling in terms  of assets instead of tasks the  orchestrator becomes an ideal system for  answering these questions that we looked  at earlier  it eliminates the friction of  translating between assets and tasks and  it can speak authoritatively about how  assets will be updated as time passes  so that gets us pretty far  but fully realizing the benefits of a  declarative approach requires going  farther  to an orchestration approach that's  based on reconciliation  here's how it  works first we observe discrepancies  between how the world is and how the  world should be  then we launch computations that are  required to make those two match  the most basic discrepancy in need of  reconciliation is when you have a  software-defined asset with no  materializations  to make the set of assets in physical  storage match the set of assets you've  declared you need to materialize the  asset  a related discrepancy is when you're  working with a partitioned asset and  only some of its partitions have  materializations  another discrepancy is when the contents  of an asset don't reflect the contents  of the upstream assets that it depends  on  so if the most recent materialization of  the asset occurred earlier than the most  recent materialization of the upstream  asset  then it's likely that the asset's  contents are stale  dagster raises its discrepancy with an  upstream changed indicator  a fourth discrepancy is when the  metadata on an asset definition doesn't  match the metadata from its latest  materialization  so here's an asset whose definition  includes metadata describing the set of  columns that it's expected to contain  every time we materialize the asset we  record the columns that were included in  the materialization  if the asset is fully reconciled then  these two lists of columns should match  but in the case up here they don't match  the definition includes a column that  the latest materialization does not  so we need to take some action oops um  so we need to take some action in this  case to resolve that  the reconciliation approach can be more  involved because it requires thinking  about all the different ways that an  asset's materializations can stray from  its definition  but it has a few big draws  so instead of trying to keep track in  your head of when assets have drifted  from their materializations you delegate  that bookkeeping to the orchestrator  you can avoid unnecessary computation so  why we compute an asset if nothing about  its inputs have changed  and you get a rationale for why  computation happened for example we  re-materialized asset x because the  columns it had no longer match the  columns that we expected it to have  so at this point here we have this  understanding of the fundamentals of  software defined assets and how we  manage change inside of them  and with that out of the way i'd like to  talk a little bit about how  software-defined assets fit into the  context of what's happened in the world  of data in the last  couple of years  if you ask 20 people for definitions of  the modern data stack you'll get at  least 20 different answers but here's a  fairly bland one that i think at least  isn't going to ruffle too many feathers  and will work for our purposes  the modern data stack is a set of tools  and practices that have dramatically  simplified common patterns for working  with data  at least in part i think that the modern  data stack owes its success to an  embrace of declarative principles for  example in the past to build a derived  table you would write an airflow task  that issues a create table statement to  your database  nowadays you're more likely to define  the table directly as a dbt  model  in the past if you wanted to ingest the  table into your data warehouse from your  production database you would write an  airflow task that executes a sync  oops  nowadays you're more likely to just tell  5tran or airbyte or miltano that you  want that table to exist in your  warehouse  with these declarative approaches you  get all the declarative benefits that we  discussed earlier so it's understandable  why they're attractive and why we're  moving in this direction  but there's something that's lost as  well  and to understand it we need to go back  in time and take a look at the  pre-modern world  in the pre-modern world you'd use an  imperative orchestrator like airflow to  invoke computations across a variety of  frameworks as we covered earlier there  are a lot of problems with this world  having an imperative orchestration layer  fosters a lot of chaos and forces you to  constantly translate between the world  of assets and the world of tasks  but there's also some important bath  water that we shouldn't throw out with  the baby here  this centralized orchestration layer  makes it easy to understand everything  that's running in one place  and to make sure that everything is  running in the right order even across  tools  in the world of the modern data stack  purpose-built tools for each of the  verticals essentially roll their own  orchestrators  so each of the tools shown here  except python which we'll get to in a  minute offers its own way to schedule  work and view the assets that are  defined inside of it  i think this loss of a central control  plan is a regression  the lack of a shared orchestration layer  results in operationally fragile data  platform  when dependencies cross tool boundaries  for example when the tables that you  derive via dbt depend on tables that are  synced from a production database  it becomes difficult to get a coherent  view of what's going on  so when dealing with this situation a  common pattern is to try to time the  schedules so that the syncs will  complete before the derived tables are  rebuilt  but this is pretty brittle if the syncs  take longer than expected there's an  overlap in the timing  and then the derived tables will get  rebuilt using stale data  so it's easy to lose track of what ran  what's supposed to ran and whether  things ran in the right order  and you might also notice that python  has largely been left behind from this  picture by focusing on specialty tools  that simplify common data patterns we  risk losing our ability to run  generalized computation when we need it  so we're forced to choose between a  a unified approach that's stuck in an  imperative paradigm  or b a declarative approach without a  unified control  plane i don't think there's any  fundamental reason we need this  trade-off we can have a declarative  asset-based orchestrator that supports  polyglot compute  dagster allows delegating computation  and asset definitions to external tools  like dbt and airbite while maintaining a  unified control plane over all of them  that control plane is declarative you  don't lose sight of the assets you're  working with when you schedule them in  your orchestrator  and it makes python a native citizen of  the modern data stack  python asset definitions can co-exist  alongside dbt models etc  so here's a dagster asset definition  graph that spans python dbt and airbyte  the three assets at the top are tables  that are populated via airbyte6  materializing them via dagser for  example clicking on that button when  we're on the asset page for one of them  results in a call to airbyte to run the  syncs  the assets in the middle with the orange  tags are dvt models  materializing them in dagster clicking  on the button you know on the asset page  results in a call to dbt either cli rbc  server or dbt cloud  and the asset with a light blue tag on  the lower right is an asset that's  computed using python downstream of one  of these dbt models  so that brings us to the end of our  journey  let's boil it down to the main things  that we talked about  the world of data needs a new spanning  abstraction which is the  software-defined asset  a software-defined asset is a  declaration in code of an asset that  should exist and how to compute it  defining assets in software enables a  new way of managing data that makes it  easier to trust easier to organize and  easier to change  the seat of the asset definition is the  orchestrator  the system that you use to manage change  should have assets as its primary  abstraction and is in the best position  to act as their source of truth  when it comes to the modern data stack  software-defined assets allow python to  become a first-class citizen  an asset-based orchestration offers a  unified control plane without  sacrificing  a declarative approach  so thank you very much i'd love to  answer any questions you have in my  office hours which i believe are  immediately after lunch
 

